(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-15"] = {"8.1":{"s":"A Bernoulli trial is a single trial with exactly two outcomes, called success and failure, in which the chance of success p stays the same every time and the trials do not affect one another. Tossing a coin, or whether a thrown die shows an even number, are examples. The chance of failure is q = 1 − p.","f":[{"x":"q = 1 - p","n":"Probability of failure, when p is the probability of success.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.245"}],"w":["Drawing without replacement is NOT a Bernoulli sequence: p changes after each draw."]},"8.2":{"s":"In n independent Bernoulli trials, the number of successes X follows a binomial distribution with parameters n and p, written X ~ B(n, p). The probability of exactly x successes is the number of ways to choose which trials succeed, times p to the power x, times q to the power n − x. These probabilities are the terms of the binomial expansion of (q + p)ⁿ. The number of trials must be fixed and the trials independent.","f":[{"x":"P(X = x) = nC_x*q^(n - x)*p^x","n":"Binomial probability function, x = 0, 1, ..., n; q = 1 - p.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.248"},{"x":"P(X >= 1) = 1 - P(X = 0)","n":"'At least one': subtract the chance of none from 1 (the book's example on p.250 uses this).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"}],"w":["For 'at least one' use the complement: 1 − qⁿ is quicker than adding many terms."]},"8.3":{"s":"For a binomial distribution B(n, p) the mean (expected value) is np and the variance is npq, where q = 1 − p. The standard deviation is the square root of the variance. These formulas save finding every probability when only the average and the spread are needed.","f":[{"x":"E(X) = mu = n*p","n":"Mean of the binomial distribution B(n, p).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"},{"x":"Var(X) = n*p*q","n":"Variance; q = 1 - p.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"},{"x":"SD(X) = sqrt(Var(X)) = sqrt(n*p*q)","n":"Standard deviation.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 2, p.250"}],"w":["The variance npq is always less than the mean np, because q < 1."]}};
